{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Moses, Aaron, and Hur.</b> Rashi explained that Hur was Miriam's son, and likewise in the section ואלה המשפטים he explained thus, that Hur was Miriam's son, and his father was Caleb, as it is said: ״ויקח לו כלב את אפרת ותלד לו את חור״ — Efrat is Miriam, as is stated in tractate Sotah. And the world objects: on the contrary, in Sotah it concludes: ״ותמת עזובה״ — this is Miriam, and why was she called Azuvah? Because everyone abandoned her. And in that very verse it is written: ״ותמת עזובה ויקח לו כלב את אפרת ותלד לו את חור״ — and if so, this [Efrat] is not Miriam, and furthermore it implies that Hur was not Miriam's son, since he was the son of Efrat. And I say that Rashi explained well, and their objection is no objection, for thus is the verse in Divrei HaYamim: ״וכלב בן חצרון הוליד את עזובה ויקח אשה את יריעות ואלה בניה ישר ושובב וארדון ותמת עזובה ויקח לו כלב את אפרת ותלד לו את חור וחור הוליד את אורי״ etc. And in Sotah, Rashi explained: Caleb son of Hetzron is he, and משנה בן יפנה is changed from the counsel of the spies. But still, he is son of Kenaz, as it is written: ״וילכדה עתניאל בן קנז אחי כלב״. Rava said: Hur was the son of Kenaz's daughter — thus it is also, for it is written ״כלב בן יפנה הקניזי״ — thus you learn. And it concludes: Azuvah — this is Miriam. And why was she called Azuvah? Because everyone abandoned her. And it seems to me that this was when she was a metzora'at. And it asks: ״הוליד את עזובה״ — but she was his wife! Rabbi Yochanan said: to teach you that anyone who marries a woman לשם שמים, the verse accounts it as though he gave birth to her." "<b>And Yeri'ot.</b> This too is Miriam, and why was she called Yeri'ot? Because her face was like white curtains. And it is written: ״ואלה בניה״ — do not read בניה but rather בוניה, meaning Caleb her husband. And he was called Yashar because he set himself straight. And Shovav, because he turned back from the counsel of the spies. And Ardon, because he subdued his inclination. And therefore I say that there he explains all the names of Caleb and all the names of Miriam — and her name [is] Miriam, and Azuvah, and Yeri'ot, and Efrat. And she was called Efrat because she was possessed of charm, like אפריון נמטייה. And I say that this is the meaning of the verse — and invert the verse and expound it: ״ותמת עזובה״ — this is Miriam; ״ויקח לו כלב את אפרת״ — that too is Miriam herself; ״ותלד לו את חור״ — before her death. And now everything fits for one who understands." "<b>Go up — Rosh.</b> Rosh: in the merit of the fathers." "<b>The hill.</b> In the merit of the mothers."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:26.996248347Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}